  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remaining centered over southern Quebec will \nbring continued cool temperatures tonight, although not quite as\nchilly as last night due to increasing high clouds. Lows are \nexpected to range from the mid 30s in the W. Adirondacks to mid \n40s in the Hudson Valley. Patchy fog will likely occur again in \nfavored sheltered valleys near bodies of water.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn ocean storm developing well east of N. Carolina today, is \nexpected to strengthen and gradually track north/west Friday \nthrough the weekend, becoming a Nor'easter. The center of the \ncyclone may eventually end up along or near the Long Island to \nNJ coast Sat into Sun, which is when the greatest impacts will \nbe in our region. For a system that is only a few days away, \nthere is still somewhat low confidence in the storm track and \nresulting rain/wind impacts across eastern NY and western New \nEngland. Overall, guidance has trended west with the storm \ntrack. However, it has to be noted there is still considerable \nspread among the deterministic and ensemble guidance. With the \nfirst half of the weekend getting into the CAMs time window, \nsome CAMs (HRRR, NAM12, RGEM) and some deterministic guidance \n(ECMWF, AI-ECMWF, GFS) favor a more westerly storm track \ncompared to the ensemble means from the ECMWF, GEFS and \ndeterministic CMC and AI-GFS, which are displaced to the east. \nEven ensembles and AI guidance have trended slightly west too.\n\nThe track will ultimately determine magnitude of impacts in our\narea. That being said, at this time we are not anticipating any\nflooding or wind headlines. Forecast rainfall has increased \ncompared to yesterday, with around 0.50-1.25\" from around \nCapital District and S. VT south and 1.25-2.00\" in SE Berkshire,\nDutchess and Litchfield counties. There is still somewhat low \nconfidence in these amounts though. Latest NBM probs for > 1.0\" \nin the 48 hr period ending 8 AM Mon have increased to around \n50-75% from the Capital District south/east, with a 20-45% \nchance of > 2.00\". Even with the increased QPF, ensemble river \nguidance is not indicating any flooding from rainfall through \nthe storm, as several days of dry/sunny weather is preceding the\nstorm. There may be a brief period of minor tidal flooding \nalong the Hudson River at Poughkeepsie Sat night into early Sun.\nSome urban/poor drainage ponding may also occur. Should the \nstorm track shift farther northwest and rainfall amounts \nincrease, the threat of more hydro impacts would increase. \n\nNortherly winds will increase on the north/west periphery of \nthe coastal storm Fri through Sat as the pressure gradient and \nwinds aloft strengthen steadily. Noting a significant inversion \nin the 3000-5000 ft layer from forecast soundings, with the \nstrongest winds aloft near or above the inversion level. So \nstrong/damaging winds not anticipated, although frequent gusts \nin the 30-40 mph range are expected in the Fri-Sun timeframe \nmainly for areas south/east of Albany. Latest NBM probabilities \nfor > 35 mph gusts are 45-70% from the E. Catskills into the \nhigher terrain of W. MA, NW. CT and the Rensselaer Plateau. The \nprobability for more impactful gusts of > 45 mph are much lower \nat 5-20% in some of these same areas, so Wind Advisories are not\nanticipated.\n\nThe cyclone will weaken on Mon, but will only slowly meander \neast. Also with the the upper trough still over the region, \nscattered showers and mostly cloudy conditions should linger.",
